  "question": {
    "text": "Which of the following statement is incorrect?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Low solubility of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LiF</mi></math> in water is due to its small hydration enthalpy.",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is paramagnetic.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Solution of sodium in liquid ammonia is conducting in nature.",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Sodium metal has higher density than potassium metal",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Due to the small size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Li</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> ion and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>ions, the lattice energy of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LiF</mi></math> is very high. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LiF</mi></math> is covalent in nature. Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>LiF</mi></math> is insoluble in water.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>KO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;&nbsp;has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> unpaired electron in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi><mo>&#8902;</mo></msup><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Molecular</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>orbital</mi></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></msubsup></math>&nbsp;. Therefore, it is Paramagnetic.</p><p>&nbsp;Lithium has lesser atomic weight than sodium. So, lithium has lesser density than sodium. But in the case of &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&#39; and &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi></math>&#39;, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></math>-orbitals present in Potassium, which increases the volume of &#39;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>&#39;.</p><p>Solution of sodium in liquid ammonia is conducting in nature, due to presence of free electron.</p></blockquote>"
